# Where every game picture comes from

Publisher artwork, used to identify the game. Decision D116: this is the
publisher's copyright and trademark in a sold, unlicensed accessory, and the
owner accepted that risk (and again on 2026-10-24: real publisher art for
every game, licensing later). Delete this folder's pictures and every config
falls back to our own banner with nothing else changed.

Rules: official publisher-provided images only. The Steam store's own
capsules for the game's app id; for a game Steam does not sell (or whose
Steam art does not name the game), the PlayStation Store's own art for the
game's concept page (the publisher uploads it, the same as a Steam capsule);
or the publisher's press kit / own site. Never Wikipedia covers, fan sites,
SteamGridDB or image search. Every row below keeps the exact URL, so a picture
can be swapped if a publisher objects.

These pictures live in the app only: the exe and the phone app carry this
folder, and nothing about a picture is ever sent to the Core (D120).

## The three shapes, and the sizes they are saved at (D121, D434; re-cut 2026-11-01)

Measured in the app (393 x 852 CSS px, dark theme, the theme-shots fixture),
2026-11-01:

| shape | where the app draws it | the box there | the picture |
|---|---|---|---|
| `wide` | the Overview banner; the Configs list; the new-config wizard's game list | about 356 x 166 CSS px (2.14:1) at most; the list and wizard tiles are smaller | Steam's **2x header capsule**, 920 x 430 (2.14:1), title included; 1024 x 576 (16:9) for the three from the PlayStation Store |
| `tall` | HOME's picture (and the More menu's small square) | about 364 x 414 CSS px on a 393 x 852 phone (0.88:1; it is `flex: 1`, so 0.75 to 0.9 across phones), with the Settings bar over its bottom 13 % | Steam's **2x vertical capsule** (`hero_capsule_2x`), 748 x 896 (0.83:1), title included; 800 x 1200 (2:3) where it comes from the PlayStation Store |
| `panel` | the simple settings menu's art panel (D433) | about 364 x 230..420 CSS px, `object-fit: cover` anchored at the top, the EXO / console chips over its top ~14 %, the config's name over it from ~53 % down | 900 x 900 JPEG cut so the title sits in the band between the two |

Every slot draws its picture WHOLE except the panel (contained over a blurred,
darkened copy of itself, .art.fit), so a picture a little off the slot's shape
gets soft edges and never loses its title.

**Why the vertical capsule for `tall` (2026-11-01).** Before this, `tall`
was Steam's 2:3 library capsule (600 x 900). HOME's box is 0.88:1, so a 2:3
cover was drawn about 276 CSS px wide with wide blurred bars each side, and at
600 px it was under 2.2x of what it was drawn at. Steam's vertical capsule is
0.83:1, nearly the box's own shape, and 748 px wide, so it fills HOME and is
sharp on a 2x phone. Warzone and Black Ops 7 already used it (their apps name
no library capsule). **PUBG, Rust and Black Ops 7** carry their title on the capsule's
foot, where HOME's Settings bar sits; their tall pictures are the whole
capsule with 150 rows of the same art blurred and darkened added below
(748 x 1046), so the title lands above the bar. No pixel of the art is
re-toned or cut.

**Why the 2x header for `wide`.** Steam's 460 x 215 header was drawn at
up to 356 CSS px, so it was soft on every 2x and 3x phone; the 2x header is
the same art at 920 x 430. Black Ops II and Black Ops 6 have no 2x header on
Steam and keep the 460 x 215 one.

**JPEG, not PNG (2026-11-01).** Every picture is now a JPEG (quality 85-86,
progressive). The old PNG covers were 0.4-1.6 MB each for photographs; the
whole folder went from about 17 MB to about 8 MB with three times the pixels
where they show. (WebP would be smaller again, but game-packs.js and its
tests accept .png and .jpg only, and that is code, not art.)

## Wide and tall pictures

Steam rows: the exact file is the `header_2x` / `hero_capsule_2x` (or
`header`) asset that Steam's own store API names for the app
(`api.steampowered.com/IStoreBrowseService/GetItems/v1` with
`"data_request": {"include_assets": true}`), read on 2026-11-01: the file is
`store_item_assets/` plus `asset_url_format` with `${FILENAME}` filled in,
without the `?t=` stamp. The hash folder changes when the publisher replaces
the art, so a row that starts answering 404 later needs the same lookup again.
