## The panel pictures (D434)

The simple settings menu (D433) draws the config's picture across everything
under its four doors, `object-fit: cover` anchored at the top, with the EXO
and console chips across its top and the config's name across its foot. A
2:3 or 0.83:1 cover cut to that box loses its title, so every pack has a
`panel` picture: a 900 x 900 JPEG cut from the publisher's own art so the
title sits whole between about 15 % and 50 % of the square's height.

The crop column says exactly what was cut, as `x, y, side` in the source's
own pixels, then scaled to 900 x 900. A negative `y` (or `x`) means the
crop starts outside the picture: those rows are the same art blurred and
darkened (the fill the app already draws behind off-shape pictures), and the
art's edge fades into them over 24 rows; a crop that runs past the bottom
does the same below. Most of the new ones (2026-11-01) are the PlayStation
Store's `MASTER` square key art (1024 x 1024), which puts the title in its
top quarter (under the chips) for most games, hence the rows added above.
Halo Infinite (not on PlayStation) and Counter-Strike 2 (PC only) are cut from
Steam's vertical capsule. **Marvel Rivals** and **Marathon** are the
concept page's title-less key art (`BACKGROUND` / `GAMEHUB_COVER_ART`) with
the same page's own `LOGO` image laid over it in the band (a soft shadow
under the logo, nothing else changed): Marvel Rivals' `MASTER` puts its logo
under the name, and Marathon's runs down the left edge under both banners.
The PlayStation Store draws its game hub the same way, from the same layers.

The rows dated before 2026-11-01 were kept as they were (their notes are in
git history, `git log -- dashboard/public/game-packs/art/SOURCES.md`).
Warzone's is Activision's own (callofduty.com's Season 02 share image).
Black Ops 7's and Black Ops 6's say "Cross-Gen Bundle" under the title
because the PlayStation Store sells the games as that bundle.

